# Lucairn config pack: setup

Gateway: `__LUCAIRN_GATEWAY_URL__`

These files hold managed settings for Claude Code and Claude Desktop
(third-party inference mode). They point both tools' model requests at your
Lucairn gateway and switch off the features listed below. IT deploys them as
managed settings, which the vendor documents as taking precedence over user
and project settings.

| File | For | Deploy with |
| - | - | - |
| `managed-settings.json` | Claude Code (terminal, VS Code / JetBrains extensions, the desktop app's Code tab) | file copy, or your MDM |
| `claude-desktop.mobileconfig` | Claude Desktop on macOS | Jamf, Kandji, Intune or any Apple MDM |
| `claude-desktop.reg` | Claude Desktop on Windows | Group Policy, Intune or any tool that writes registry policy |

**As downloaded, the files contain no Lucairn key.** IT supplies each user's
key: in the Claude Desktop profile before deployment, and for Claude Code
through a helper command named in `managed-settings.json` (see "Credentials"
below).

## 1. Claude Code: `managed-settings.json`

Place the file, unchanged, at:

- macOS: `/Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode/managed-settings.json`
- Linux and WSL: `/etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json`
- Windows: `C:\Program Files\ClaudeCode\managed-settings.json`

Or deliver the same keys through MDM: the macOS preference domain
`com.anthropic.claudecode`, or the `Settings` value (`REG_SZ`, the whole JSON
document) under `HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\ClaudeCode`. Claude Code uses only the
highest-ranked managed source that is present, so use one mechanism per machine.

The file sets `"requiredMinimumVersion": "2.1.285"`, and this is not
optional: `allowedProviders` needs Claude Code 2.1.285 or later, and an older
version would start without that lock. The vendor documents that with this
key "Claude Code exits at startup and tells the user to update" when the
running version is older; `claude update`, `claude install` and
`claude doctor` keep working so users can recover. Two limits, both from the
vendor's documentation: the key itself needs Claude Code 2.1.163 or later, so
a version older than that ignores it and starts without either lock (remove
such versions from your devices); and the check runs only at startup, so a
session that was already running keeps going until it is restarted. The Code
tab in Claude Desktop runs its own copy of Claude Code: update Claude Desktop
on a test device first and check that Code-tab sessions still start.

What the settings do (as the vendor documents each key):

- `apiKeyHelper`: the command that gives Claude Code the user's Lucairn key.
  As downloaded it is the placeholder `REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_KEY_HELPER_COMMAND`;
  see "Credentials" below.
- `env.ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` + `allowedProviders: ["customEndpoint"]`: sessions
  use `__LUCAIRN_GATEWAY_URL__`, and Claude Code refuses a session pointed
  anywhere else, including the vendor's own API.
- `requiredMinimumVersion: "2.1.285"`: Claude Code 2.1.163 to 2.1.284 won't
  start; versions before 2.1.163 ignore the key (see above).
- `env.ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS` adds `x-lucairn-require-added-parts-sanitized: 1`
  to each request. A Lucairn gateway that supports this header answers HTTP
  412 (`added_parts_sanitization_unavailable`) before forwarding anything when
  it can't scan the text Claude Code adds on its own (pulled-in files,
  `CLAUDE.md`, command and skill text). An older gateway that doesn't know
  the header doesn't refuse on it.
- `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_GIT_INSTRUCTIONS=1` + `includeGitInstructions: false`:
  leaves out the git status block (branch names, commit subjects, git user
  name) that Claude Code otherwise adds to the first message.
- `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AUTO_MEMORY=1` + `autoMemoryEnabled: false`: auto memory
  off.
- `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_NONESSENTIAL_TRAFFIC`, `DISABLE_TELEMETRY`,
  `DISABLE_ERROR_REPORTING`, `DISABLE_FEEDBACK_COMMAND`: telemetry, error
  reporting, feedback uploads and automatic updates off. IT distributes
  updates.
- `skipWebFetchPreflight: true`: WebFetch skips its host-name check at
  `api.anthropic.com`. Restrict domains with `WebFetch` permission rules if you
  need to.
- `disableClaudeAiConnectors: true`: claude.ai connectors off.
- `disableAutoMode: "disable"`, `useAutoModeDuringPlan: false`,
  `permissions.defaultMode: "default"`: auto mode off. In auto mode Claude Code
  adds a separate field to each request, outside the messages, with the
  working folder, home folder and user name.
- `permissions.blockReadsOutsideWorkingDirectories: true` and the `Read(...)`
  deny rules: Claude's file tools are kept to the opened folders and denied
  common credential files (`~/.ssh`, cloud CLI folders, `.env*`, key files).
  The file-name rules start with `//` (for example `Read(//**/.env)`), which
  the vendor documents as "any `.env` anywhere on the filesystem", so they
  also hold in folders added with `--add-dir`; a rule without the `//` would
  only cover the folder the session started in.
  Deny rules cover Claude's file tools and the shell commands Claude Code
  recognises; they are not an operating-system sandbox.
- `allowManagedHooksOnly`, `allowManagedMcpServersOnly` + `allowedMcpServers: []`,
  `strictKnownMarketplaces: []`, `disableSideloadFlags`, and the built-in
  guard's `allowManagedModsOnly`: hooks and MCP servers are limited to the ones
  your organisation deploys, and user-added marketplaces, plugins, mods, MCP
  servers and side-loading command-line flags are blocked. With
  `allowManagedHooksOnly` set in managed settings, mods inside plugins stop too,
  your own included (mods built into Claude Code keep running). List your own
  marketplaces or servers in these keys if you have any.
- `parentSettingsBehavior: "merge"`: in the desktop app's Code tab, Claude
  Desktop's restrictions apply on top of this file instead of being dropped.

Check one machine: run `/status` in Claude Code and open the Status tab.
"Setting sources" must list the managed settings, and "Anthropic base URL"
must show `__LUCAIRN_GATEWAY_URL__`.

### Credentials (Claude Code)

As downloaded, the file sets
`"apiKeyHelper": "REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_KEY_HELPER_COMMAND"` (a pack made with
the kit's `--key-helper` option already names your command). Before you
deploy it, replace the placeholder with a command that prints the user's
Lucairn key, for example from your secrets manager. Claude Code runs
the command through the system shell and sends its output as `X-Api-Key`
(the header the gateway reads) and as `Authorization: Bearer`. While the
placeholder is in place, the helper fails, and Claude Code sends its requests
to the gateway with an empty key (measured).

The helper is the only credential option this pack supports. With a key in
`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` or a token in `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, Claude Code
also sends `GET https://api.anthropic.com/api/claude_code/settings` with that
credential at every start, straight to the vendor and not through the
gateway (measured with Claude Code 2.1.287). The vendor documents the
exception the pack relies on: "Neither keys returned by an `apiKeyHelper`
script nor Workload Identity Federation credentials trigger the settings
fetch." Neither the pinned `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` nor
`CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_NONESSENTIAL_TRAFFIC` stops that request (measured).

- Don't put a key or token into the file's `env` block (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`,
  `ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN`, `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`); the pack's checker
  refuses a file that sets any of them. Don't put the key into
  `ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS` or any other `env` value either: those are plain
  text in the file and are passed to every program Claude Code starts.
- A user can still export a credential in their own shell. With a key in
  `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` (which the vendor ranks above `apiKeyHelper`) or a
  token in `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, Claude Code sends that start-up request
  again, with that credential (measured); model requests stay on the
  gateway. The firewall block on `api.anthropic.com` (section 3) is the
  backstop for this case.
- `claude --bare` doesn't use the helper named in this file and stops with
  "Not logged in" (measured). The vendor documents the way for bare mode:
  "supply an `apiKeyHelper` in the `--settings` JSON".
- Users can still start a Claude account sign-in (`/login` or
  `claude auth login`); the pack doesn't block it. Model requests stay on the
  gateway: the base URL is pinned, and the vendor ranks `apiKeyHelper` above
  a `/login` sign-in (measured: after the sign-in was opened and cancelled,
  the next request went to the gateway; a completed sign-in was not tested).
  Blocking `claude.ai`, `claude.com` and `platform.claude.com` (section 3)
  should stop the sign-in itself (not tested).
- Don't add `forceLoginMethod`, `forceLoginOrgUUID` or
  `forceLoginGatewayUrl`: the vendor documents that they block gateway
  credentials at startup.

## 2. Claude Desktop: `.mobileconfig` (macOS) and `.reg` (Windows)

Before you deploy, fill in two things:

1. **Credential.** Replace `REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_LUCAIRN_KEY` in
   `inferenceGatewayApiKey` with the user's Lucairn key. The profile sets
   `inferenceCredentialKind` to `static`; the vendor documents for that key:
   "When set, only that source is used (no fallback)." Never put a key in
   `inferenceCustomHeaders`: that map is plain configuration.
   `inferenceGatewayAuthScheme` is `x-api-key`, the header the gateway reads.

   Claude Desktop can also run a credential helper program, set up as the
   vendor describes at
   https://claude.com/docs/third-party/claude-desktop/configuration#inferencecredentialhelper.
   This pack doesn't use one: a helper can print request headers as well as
   the key, and the vendor documents that those are "merged over these static
   entries (helper wins on conflict)", so a helper could send
   `x-lucairn-require-added-parts-sanitized: 0` and switch off the check
   described above. The static key is the supported path.
2. **Models.** The Lucairn gateway does not serve `GET /v1/models`, so Claude
   Desktop can't fill its model picker by itself. Add `inferenceModels` with
   the full model IDs your organisation uses, as a JSON array written as one
   string, for example `["model-id-1","model-id-2"]`. Without it the picker is
   empty.

Keep `inferenceGatewayBaseUrl` exactly equal to `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` in
`managed-settings.json`: `allowedProviders` accepts only that exact value.

Deploy:

- macOS: upload `claude-desktop.mobileconfig` to your MDM (preference domain
  `com.anthropic.claudefordesktop`).
- Windows: import `claude-desktop.reg` (as administrator) or deploy the same
  values with your tool. Every value is `REG_SZ`, directly under
  `HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\Claude`. The app ignores `HKCU\SOFTWARE\Policies\Claude`
  completely when machine policy is present, so keep the whole configuration
  in one hive.
- Users must quit and reopen Claude Desktop after a change.

What the profile sets: gateway mode with the require header above; the
static key as the credential source (`inferenceCredentialKind`, which the
vendor documents as "no fallback"); the
claude.ai sign-in option hidden (`disableDeploymentModeChooser`); auto mode
off (`autoModeEnabled: false`); file tools kept to the working folders
(`blockReadsOutsideWorkingDirectories`); the WebFetch host-name check skipped
(`skipWebFetchPreflight`); user-added MCP servers, desktop extensions, plugin
marketplaces, plugin uploads and user-created skills off; crash reports,
analytics and non-essential third-party fetches off; app updates read from
`releases.claude.com` instead of `api.anthropic.com` (`updateViaUpdatesHost`).
Each key needs a minimum Claude Desktop version, listed per key ("Added in")
in the vendor's configuration reference; check it against the builds on your
devices. Test the profile on one device before rolling it out.

To pin a proxy, add `egressProxyUrl` (MDM only); model requests then reach the
gateway through that proxy.

## 3. Firewall

- **Allow** from user devices: `__LUCAIRN_GATEWAY_HOST__`, TCP port __LUCAIRN_GATEWAY_PORT__ (HTTPS).
- **Block** from user devices: `api.anthropic.com` (the vendor's model API;
  also WebFetch host-name checks, feature flags, telemetry and the start-up
  settings request described under "Credentials") and
  `mcp-proxy.anthropic.com` (claude.ai connectors). With the helper as its
  credential, Claude Code reached no host but the gateway in our test
  (Claude Code 2.1.287 on Linux, against a stand-in gateway that logged every
  other host name). The block is the backstop for what the files can't
  prevent: a user who exports a key or token in their own shell, a machine
  where the pack is missing or was removed, and possibly a completed Team or
  Enterprise sign-in (the vendor lists it as a credential for that request;
  not tested with the helper in place). With the block in place, a start
  that tries that request waits for it to fail (about 17 seconds per start
  when the firewall refuses the connection; one that silently drops packets
  may make it wait longer), and an interactive session shows "Remote managed
  settings failed to load".
- **Keep** `downloads.claude.ai` reachable: Claude Desktop fetches its
  workspace bundle and the Claude Code engine from it when a session starts
  (unless you install the offline installer), and Claude Code's installer and
  Claude Desktop's updates use it. Keep `releases.claude.com` too if Claude
  Desktop should keep updating itself; set `disableAutoUpdates` instead if IT
  pushes every version.
- **Can be blocked**: hosts used by features these keys switch off, for
  example `*.sentry.io`, `sentry.io`, `http-intake.logs.us5.datadoghq.com`,
  `browser-intake-datadoghq.com` and its regional variants, `www.google.com`
  and `*.gstatic.com` (connector icons), `www.claudeusercontent.com` and
  `*.claudemcpcontent.com` (preview frames).
- Blocking `claude.ai`, `claude.com` and `platform.claude.com` should also
  stop claude.ai sign-in inside Claude Code (not tested); the gateway
  credential replaces it.
  Whether users may still reach claude.ai in a browser is your decision.

## 4. Requirements and limits

- The pack sends only the Lucairn key, so the gateway must hold the model
  provider credential: a self-hosted Lucairn gateway, or a hosted Pro or
  Enterprise account whose provider key is stored on the gateway (Keys page).
  An account that sends its provider key with every request can't use this
  pack for Claude Code.
- A 412 with `added_parts_sanitization_unavailable` means the gateway refused
  the request before forwarding it, because it couldn't confirm that the text
  Claude Code adds would be scanned.
- Codex and other tools are not covered by this pack.
- The pack leaves your own audit export unset. To send activity to your own
  OpenTelemetry collector, add `CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_TELEMETRY=1` and the
  `OTEL_*` exporter variables (https://code.claude.com/docs/en/monitoring-usage)
  to the Claude Code `env` block, and `otlpEndpoint` to the Claude Desktop
  profile.

